WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.229 --> 00:00:06.410
Now that we know how to send a variable over the network. Let's take a look at how we can send a function of the network

2
00:00:07.429 --> 00:00:12.470
much like with propagating variables the reflection system does a lot of the heavy lifting here a

3
00:00:13.789 --> 00:00:20.210
Remote procedure call or RPC is a method of calling a function on one or more remote clients or the server

4
00:00:20.789 --> 00:00:24.570
We can go from the client to the server or from the server to a specific client

5
00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:29.250
As long as we're calling the function from an object that's ultimately owned by the player controller

6
00:00:29.769 --> 00:00:36.299
Each player controller represents the network's connection between the client and the controlling server with no player controller

7
00:00:36.299 --> 00:00:39.619
We're unable to pick out a particular client to send a message to

8
00:00:41.429 --> 00:00:47.549
Multicasts however are sent from the server to every connected client and as such require no player controller to be involved

9
00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:53.899
Defining a function to be called over the network is as easy as flagging a function with the U function markup and

10
00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:57.560
Supplying the appropriate specifiers in our case here

11
00:00:57.560 --> 00:01:03.280
We've got a multicast function being sent out reliably and with some required validation when calling it

12
00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:05.980
We'll cover the multicast in just a second

13
00:01:05.980 --> 00:01:11.260
But a quick note on reliability and validation if we call an RPC reliably

14
00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:16.579
We flag into the engine that this function must go through to receiving clients. It doesn't matter if it's late

15
00:01:16.579 --> 00:01:22.379
It just needs to reach them as gameplay engineers. We can't just ignore a gameplay critical function call

16
00:01:22.379 --> 00:01:26.659
So flagging a function as reliable ensures that the function will be called regardless of lag

17
00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:33.219
We can also flag something as unreliable which essentially outlines functions that are cosmetic or non gameplay critical

18
00:01:33.219 --> 00:01:36.299
If we hit a patch of lag just after calling this function

19
00:01:36.299 --> 00:01:40.260
We don't need to worry about it being received as much as we would with a reliable function

20
00:01:42.319 --> 00:01:46.500
Validation allows us to assess whether or not a networked function is what we expect it to be

21
00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:51.620
If we return false from the validate function, the requesting client will be booted from the server

22
00:01:51.959 --> 00:01:57.079
So consider the following the client presses reload sending a reload call to the server

23
00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:01.760
If the amount that they want to reload by in a single call is greater than say a single bullet

24
00:02:01.959 --> 00:02:07.420
We can assume they found some kind of exploits or work around and boot them from the server before they have any meaningful impact

25
00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:13.340
As for the actual function definition, we actually have three separate functions that we need to implement

26
00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:19.919
Firstly, we've got our base you function written here as multicast to do function. This is just a stub function

27
00:02:19.919 --> 00:02:21.919
It doesn't require any implementation directly

28
00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:24.780
Then we have an explicitly defined

29
00:02:24.979 --> 00:02:29.900
Implementation function for the networking subsystem to call once it receives the call over the network

30
00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:34.939
And this is the main function where we put our gameplay code. This is the body that contains our implementation

31
00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:43.030
We then have a validate function that holds our RPC validation and certification code as we mentioned above if we omit the with

32
00:02:43.189 --> 00:02:47.069
Validation specifier the compiler won't prompt us to add a validate function

33
00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:50.460
RPCs come in three flavors

34
00:02:51.139 --> 00:02:56.659
First we've got server functions and this function when called on the client will execute the body on the server

35
00:02:57.139 --> 00:03:02.020
This will only work if the object that we're calling the RPC from is owned by the local player controller

36
00:03:03.169 --> 00:03:08.110
Next we've got client when called on the server. The body of this function will be executed client-side

37
00:03:08.979 --> 00:03:14.180
Much like a server call a client call also needs to be called from an object owned by a player controller

38
00:03:14.539 --> 00:03:18.460
This player controller dictates what client the body is actually executed on

39
00:03:20.090 --> 00:03:22.889
Lastly we've got multicast and this function is fired everywhere

40
00:03:22.969 --> 00:03:26.650
That's locally on the server as well as on all relevant clients

41
00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:31.110
If the client is out of relevancy, the RPC will still be sent but not received
